Task 1: Configure Network Policies for VMs
Subtask 1.1: Verify Virtualization Environment
First, let's confirm that our virtualization environment is properly set up and ready for network policy configuration.

Check virtualization support:
# Verify CPU virtualization support
lscpu | grep Virtualization

# Check if KVM modules are loaded
lsmod | grep kvm

# Verify libvirt service status
systemctl status libvirtd
Install required packages (if not already present):
# Update package repository
sudo apt update

# Install virtualization and network tools
sudo apt install -y qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients bridge-utils virt-manager iptables-persistent

# Start and enable libvirt service
sudo systemctl start libvirtd
sudo systemctl enable libvirtd
Add user to libvirt group:
# Add current user to libvirt group
sudo usermod -a -G libvirt $USER

# Apply group changes (logout/login or use newgrp)
newgrp libvirt
Subtask 1.2: Create Virtual Networks
Now we'll create separate virtual networks that we can apply policies to.

Create the first virtual network (Production Network):
# Create production network XML configuration
cat > production-network.xml << EOF
<network>
  <name>production-net</name>
  <forward mode='nat'/>
  <bridge name='virbr-prod' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <ip address='192.168.100.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='192.168.100.10' end='192.168.100.50'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
EOF

# Define and start the production network
virsh net-define production-network.xml
virsh net-start production-net
virsh net-autostart production-net
Create the second virtual network (Development Network):
# Create development network XML configuration
cat > development-network.xml << EOF
<network>
  <name>development-net</name>
  <forward mode='nat'/>
  <bridge name='virbr-dev' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <ip address='192.168.200.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='192.168.200.10' end='192.168.200.50'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
EOF

# Define and start the development network
virsh net-define development-network.xml
virsh net-start development-net
virsh net-autostart development-net
Verify network creation:
# List all virtual networks
virsh net-list --all

# Show network details
virsh net-info production-net
virsh net-info development-net

# Check bridge interfaces
ip addr show | grep virbr
