Task 2: Implement Isolation Between VM Networks
Subtask 2.1: Configure Basic Network Isolation
Now we'll implement network policies to control traffic between our VM networks.

Check current iptables rules:
# View current iptables rules
sudo iptables -L -n -v

# Check NAT rules
sudo iptables -t nat -L -n -v

# Check FORWARD chain specifically
sudo iptables -L FORWARD -n -v
Create custom iptables chain for VM policies:
# Create custom chain for VM network policies
sudo iptables -N VM_NETWORK_POLICY

# Insert rule to jump to our custom chain
sudo iptables -I FORWARD -j VM_NETWORK_POLICY

# Verify the chain was created
sudo iptables -L VM_NETWORK_POLICY -n -v
Implement default deny policy between networks:
# Block traffic from production network to development network
sudo iptables -A VM_NETWORK_POLICY -s 192.168.100.0/24 -d 192.168.200.0/24 -j DROP

# Block traffic from development network to production network
sudo iptables -A VM_NETWORK_POLICY -s 192.168.200.0/24 -d 192.168.100.0/24 -j DROP

# Allow established and related connections (for return traffic)
sudo iptables -A VM_NETWORK_POLICY -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# Log dropped packets for monitoring
sudo iptables -A VM_NETWORK_POLICY -j LOG --log-prefix "VM_POLICY_DROP: " --log-level 4

# Verify rules are in place
sudo iptables -L VM_NETWORK_POLICY -n -v
Subtask 2.2: Configure Selective Access Rules
Let's create more granular policies that allow specific types of communication while maintaining security.

Allow specific services between networks:
# Allow SSH (port 22) from production to development for management
sudo iptables -I VM_NETWORK_POLICY 1 -s 192.168.100.0/24 -d 192.168.200.0/24 -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Allow HTTP (port 80) from development to production for testing
sudo iptables -I VM_NETWORK_POLICY 2 -s 192.168.200.0/24 -d 192.168.100.0/24 -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT

# Allow ping (ICMP) for network diagnostics
sudo iptables -I VM_NETWORK_POLICY 3 -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -I VM_NETWORK_POLICY 4 -p icmp --icmp-type echo-reply -j ACCEPT

# View updated rules
sudo iptables -L VM_NETWORK_POLICY -n -v --line-numbers
Create time-based access rules:
# Allow database access (port 3306) only during business hours
sudo iptables -I VM_NETWORK_POLICY 5 -s 192.168.200.0/24 -d 192.168.100.0/24 -p tcp --dport 3306 -m time --timestart 09:00 --timestop 17:00 --weekdays Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri -j ACCEPT

# Verify time-based rule
sudo iptables -L VM_NETWORK_POLICY -n -v --line-numbers
Save iptables rules for persistence:
# Save current iptables rules
sudo iptables-save > /tmp/vm-network-policies.rules

# Install rules to persist across reboots
sudo cp /tmp/vm-network-policies.rules /etc/iptables/rules.v4

# Verify rules are saved
cat /etc/iptables/rules.v4 | grep VM_NETWORK_POLICY
Subtask 2.3: Configure Network Monitoring
Set up monitoring to track policy effectiveness and network traffic patterns.

Enable detailed logging:
# Create log monitoring script
cat > /tmp/monitor-vm-traffic.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== VM Network Policy Monitor ==="
echo "Monitoring network traffic and policy violations..."
echo "Press Ctrl+C to stop"
echo

# Monitor iptables logs
tail -f /var/log/kern.log | grep "VM_POLICY_DROP" &
TAIL_PID=$!

# Monitor network connections
while true; do
    echo "=== Active Connections $(date) ==="
    ss -tuln | grep -E "(192.168.100|192.168.200)"
    echo
    sleep 10
done

# Cleanup on exit
trap "kill $TAIL_PID 2>/dev/null" EXIT
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/monitor-vm-traffic.sh
Create traffic analysis script:
# Create traffic analysis script
cat > /tmp/analyze-vm-traffic.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== VM Network Traffic Analysis ==="
echo

# Show iptables rule statistics
echo "Policy Rule Statistics:"
sudo iptables -L VM_NETWORK_POLICY -n -v

echo
echo "Network Interface Statistics:"
cat /proc/net/dev | grep -E "(virbr-prod|virbr-dev)"

echo
echo "Recent Policy Violations:"
sudo grep "VM_POLICY_DROP" /var/log/kern.log | tail -10

echo
echo "Active VM Network Connections:"
sudo netstat -i | grep -E "(virbr-prod|virbr-dev)"
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/analyze-vm-traffic.sh
